---
import Sub from '../layouts/Sub.astro';
import { cityPageSchema, SERVICE_PAGES } from '../data/site.js';

const description =
  'Concrete sealing in Santa Clara, Utah — restoration and sealing for established neighborhoods, plus driveways, patios and pool decks in the newer builds. Free on-site assessment.';

const schema = cityPageSchema({
  path: '/concrete-sealing-santa-clara-ut',
  city: 'Santa Clara',
  description,
});
---

<Sub
  title="Concrete Sealing in Santa Clara, UT | Armour Crete"
  description={description}
  crumb="Santa Clara, UT"
  schema={schema}
>
  <Fragment slot="h1">Concrete sealing<br />in Santa Clara.</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    Santa Clara has some of the oldest streets in the county and some of the
    newest — which means slabs at every stage of their life. The older ones
    usually need honest prep before anything goes down; the newer ones are at
    the perfect moment to be protected. We handle both.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="aside">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Prep</dt><dd>First, always — nothing goes down over a problem</dd></div>
      <div><dt>24h</dt><dd>Cure before the surface is back in use</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Free</dt><dd>On-site assessment, anywhere in Santa Clara</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Services in Santa Clara, UT</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Everything we do,<br />done here.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="chips rv">
        {SERVICE_PAGES.map((s) => <li><a href={s.href}>{s.label}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
      <div class="prose rv" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <p>
          On the established streets the work often starts with correction —
          <a class="tlink" href="/expansion-joint-replacement">failed joints
          re-filled</a>, cracks patched, surfaces cleaned back to sound concrete
          — because sealer over damage just locks the damage in. On the newer
          builds toward the hills it is the opposite job: young slabs, fully
          cured, sealed before the desert has taken anything from them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-dark">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-lt">Older slabs, honest answers</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Restore what's worth<br />restoring. Say so when it isn't.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose rv">
        <p>
          Decades of sun, sprinklers and winter nights leave their record in a
          slab — scaling at the edges, joints gone crumbly, the white bloom of
          efflorescence where water has been moving through. Most of it can be
          corrected and sealed, and the result is a surface that stops getting
          worse. Some of it is past that, and the honest call is replacement
          before protection.
        </p>
        <p>
          That call is what the free assessment is for. We tell you which side of
          the line your concrete is on, what the prep would involve, and what the
          sealed surface will and will not look like afterwards — on your own
          slab, not a brochure's.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Santa Clara questions</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Asked in<br />Santa Clara.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq rv">
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Our driveway is thirty years old. Is sealing even worth it?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Often, yes — after correction. If the surface is sound or can be made
            sound, sealing stops the decline where it is. If it is past saving we
            will say so plainly, because sealing a failed slab wastes your money
            and our name.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Trees shade half our patio. Does that change anything?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            It changes the wear pattern — shaded concrete and full-sun concrete
            age at very different rates, and organic debris holds moisture
            against the surface. Same four-step process, but the product choice
            and the maintenance cycle take the shade into account.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>How long before we can use the surface again?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Twenty-four hours after the sealer goes down. One day, and it is
            yours again.
          </p></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Sub>
